    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nThe handlers in `ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` already built `ProblemDetail` objects (which serialize the four required members), but they were sent without the `application/problem+json` content type \u2014 Spring's default converter emitted `application/json`. The new acceptance test (`coreRejectionIsProblemJson`) asserts the exact content type.\n\nI fixed this by setting `.contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_PROBLEM_JSON)` on every rejection response builder for the three affected statuses:\n\n- **409** \u2014 the shared `conflict(...)` helper (duplicate identity, city at capacity)\n- **400** \u2014 the shared `invalidField(...)` helper (invalid postcode, disposable email), plus the `MethodArgumentNotValidException` handler (both return points), `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, and `FutureRegistrationDateException`\n- **429** \u2014 `DailyOwnerRegistrationLimitException`\n\nI left the 404 (`DataIntegrityViolationException`) and 500 (`handleGeneralException`) handlers unchanged, since the spec scopes the requirement to the 400/409/429 rejections, and verified those are the only paths \u2014 all such rejections flow through this advice via thrown exceptions; the controller only returns 404/2xx directly. HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
